  "text": "A method was tested in which bubble‑filled tubes were used to pull out surface‑active chemicals from water. By keeping track of how much material entered, left, and stuck to the bubble surfaces, a simple calculation was created that can forecast how well the device works under different flow rates and concentrations. Experiments were carried out with a typical anionic detergent in a dilute water solution. The amount of chemical that could attach to the bubble surface, measured directly, matched the amount predicted from surface‑tension data. The model also correctly anticipated how changes in liquid feed speed, gas flow, and initial concentration would affect removal efficiency. When the liquid feed was added to the bottom liquid pool, the system behaved like a single equilibrium step, described by an operating‑line equation. Introducing the feed directly into the rising foam created a series of such steps, offering the possibility of improved performance through multiple stages."
